%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F7680250] ## What is Statics?
%[text] To understand the state of any physical system, we use Newton's laws of motion. Informally, we can state the laws as follows:
%[text] 1. A force is required to change the motion of an object.
%[text] 2. Force = mass \* acceleration
%[text] 3. When two objects interact, they exert equal and opposite forces on each other. \
%[text] A static system is a system which is at rest. This means that all forces in the system must be exactly balanced by other forces in the system. Since we can represent forces as vectors, to study such systems we will draw a free-body diagram including the objects and forces in a system and use linear algebra to draw conclusions about the implications of Newton's laws on physical systems. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8B0610C5] ## Decomposing Forces
%[text] Rather than adding up all the vectors at once, it is very common to consider a single direction at a time. This requires decomposing our forces into components in each direction. This can also allow us to move between different spatial representations such as cartesian, polar, or spherical. We will use unit vectors to track direction, and vector addition to combine each of the forces and identify the resultant force.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_A96C2419] Consider the vector $\\vec{A} = (A\_x,A\_y,A\_z)${"altText":"vector(A) = (A\_x,A\_y,A\_z)"}. It forms an angle of $\\gamma${"altText":"gamma"} with the $z${"altText":"z"}-axis, an angle of $\\beta${"altText":"beta"} with the $y${"altText":"y"}-axis and an angle of $\\alpha${"altText":"alpha"} with the $x${"altText":"x"}-axis. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A vector decomposition diagram showing the projection of a vector A onto its Cartesian coordinates: A\_x in the x direction, A\_y in the y direction and A\_z in the z direction.](text:image:723d)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_2FE1EC59] Let $\\vec{u}\_A${"altText":"vector(u)\_A"} be the unit vector in the direction of $\\vec{A}${"altText":"vector(A)"}. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_872E9CFC] $\\vec{A} = ||A||\\cdot \\vec{u\_A}${"altText":"vector(A) = ||A|| dot vector(u\_A)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_A86D242A] $\\vec{u}\_A = \[u\_x,u\_y,u\_z\]${"altText":"vector(u)\_A = \[u\_x,u\_y,u\_z\]"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $||\\vec{u}\_A|| = 1${"altText":"||vector(u)\_A|| = 1"}
%[text] The corresponding unit vector $u\_A${"altText":"u\_A"} can be calculated by knowing two points along $A${"altText":"A"},
%[text] $p\_1 = (x\_1, y\_1, z\_1)${"altText":"p\_1 = (x\_1, y\_1, z\_1)"} and  $p\_2 = (x\_2, y\_2, z\_2)${"altText":"p\_2 = (x\_2, y\_2, z\_2)"}. The the distance between $p\_1${"altText":"p\_1"} and $p\_2${"altText":"p\_2"} is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_13FD6DAC] $d=\\sqrt{{\\left(x\_2 -x\_1 \\right)}^2 +{\\left(y\_2 -y\_1 \\right)}^2 +{\\left(z\_2 -z\_1 \\right)}^2 }${"editStyle":"visual"} $= ||p\_2-p\_1||${"altText":"= ||p\_2-p\_1||"}
%[text] so the unit vector in the direction of $A${"altText":"A"} is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_84C3B65D] $u\_A = \\left\[ \\frac{x\_2-x\_1}{d},\\frac{y\_2-y\_1}{d},\\frac{z\_2-z\_1}{d}\\right\] = \\frac{\\vec{A}}{||A||} = \\frac{p\_2-p\_1}{||p\_2-p\_1||}${"altText":"u\_A = \[ (x\_2-x\_1)\/(d),(y\_2-y\_1)\/(d),(z\_2-z\_1)\/(d)\] = (vector(A))\/(||A||) = (p\_2-p\_1)\/(||p\_2-p\_1||)"}.
%[text] In the case shown above, where the origin can be taken as $p\_1${"altText":"p\_1"} and $(A\_x,A\_y,A\_z)${"altText":"(A\_x,A\_y,A\_z)"} can be taken as $p\_2${"altText":"p\_2"}, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u\_A = \\left\[\\frac{A\_x}{\\sqrt{A\_x^2+A\_y^2+A\_z^2}},\\frac{A\_y}{\\sqrt{A\_x^2+A\_y^2+A\_z^2}},\\frac{A\_z}{\\sqrt{A\_x^2+A\_y^2+A\_z^2}}\\right\]${"altText":"u\_A = \[(A\_x)\/(sqrt(A\_x^2+A\_y^2+A\_z^2)),(A\_y)\/(sqrt(A\_x^2+A\_y^2+A\_z^2)),(A\_z)\/(sqrt(A\_x^2+A\_y^2+A\_z^2))\]"}.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_92BB508C] ## Compute the Magnitude and Direction of a Resultant Force
%[text] Determine the magnitude and direction of the resultant force on the hook at point $A${"altText":"A"}. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A force diagram with a rope hooked at point A being pulled toward point B with force F\_AB and towards point C with force F\_AC. The point B is the origin. The point C is located in the X-Y plane a distance d\_1 from the X-axis and d\_2 from the Y-axis. The point A is in the Y-Z plane a distance d\_3 from the Y axis and d\_4 from the Z axis.](text:image:7de4)
%[text] Say we know the measured distances: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $d\_1 = 8 \\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_1 = 8 m"},      $d\_2 = 4 \\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_2 = 4 m"},      $d\_3 = 4 \\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_3 = 4 m"},      and      $d\_4 = 2 \\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_4 = 2 m"}.
%[text] We can identify the points: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $A = (0,2,4)${"altText":"A = (0,2,4)"}, $B = (0,0,0)${"altText":"B = (0,0,0)"}, and $C=(4,8,0)${"altText":"C=(4,8,0)"}. 
%[text] To work out the case of the force $F\_{AB}${"altText":"F\_AB"} by hand:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vec{AB} = B-A = (0,-2,-4)${"altText":"vector(AB) = B-A = (0,-2,-4)"} and $d\_{AB} = \\sqrt{0^2+(-2)^2+(-4)^2} = \\sqrt{20}${"altText":"d\_AB = sqrt(0^2+(-2)^2+(-4)^2) = sqrt(20)"}.
%[text] Therefore,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vec{u\_{AB}} = \\frac{\\vec{AB}}{d\_{AB}} = \\frac{\\vec{AB}}{\\left|\\left|B-A\\right|\\right|}= \\left(0,\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{20}}, \\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{20}}\\right)${"altText":"vector(u\_AB) = (vector(AB))\/(d\_AB) = (vector(AB))\/(||B-A||)= (0,(-2)\/(sqrt(20)), (-4)\/(sqrt(20)))"},
%[text] and, if the magnitude of the force towards $B${"altText":"B"} is $600${"altText":"600"} Newtons:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vec{F\_{AB}} = 600\\cdot \\vec{u\_{AB}} = 600\\left(0,\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{20}}, \\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{20}}\\right)${"altText":"vector(F\_AB) = 600 dot vector(u\_AB) = 600(0,(-2)\/(sqrt(20)), (-4)\/(sqrt(20)))"}.
%[text] We could solve this question numerically as well:
% Define the points and forces of interest
A = [0 2 4];
B = [0 0 0];
C = [4 8 0];

% Identify the magnitudes of each force
F_AB = 600;
F_AC = 500;

% Decompose the force AB
AB = B-A;
u_AB = AB/norm(AB);
F_AB = u_AB*F_AB %[output:353b7717]

% Decompose the force AC
AC = C-A;
u_AC = AC/norm(AC);
F_AC = u_AC*F_AC %[output:9ed6693a]

% Calculate the resultant force
F_R = F_AB + F_AC %[output:2cc24a5b]

% Identify the direction of the resultant force
u_FR = F_R/norm(F_R) %[output:7fbaa034]
%[text] ![](text:image:9a86) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. How does your resultant force change if you change the locations of $A${"altText":"A"}, $B${"altText":"B"}, and $C${"altText":"C"}?
%[text] 2. How does your resultant force change if you change the magnitudes of the forces $F\_{AB}${"altText":"F\_AB"} and $F\_{AC}${"altText":"F\_AC"}? \
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3CA1AB60] ### ![](text:image:463f) **Exercise 1: Calculate Forces in 3 Dimensions**
%[text] A trap door is held open by two chains, as depicted below. Express the given forces as cartesian vectors.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![There is a cartesian axis with positive X pointing left, positive Y pointing right, and positive Z pointing up. When closed, the trap door is located in the X-Y plane with the hinges paralleling the X-axis at a distance of d1 in the negative Y direction. The door is open an angle of theta in the positive Z direction.The width of the door is d3. The length of the door is d4.  The open corner of the door along the Y-axis is labeled A and connected by a chain to the origin. The other open corner is labeled C and is connected by a chain to B located a distance d2 in the negative X direction. ](text:image:77d4)
%[text] Choose values for the measurements in the diagram:
% Distances
d_1 = 1;   % meters
d_2 = 0.5; % meters
d_3 = 1.5; % meters
d_4 = 2.5; % meters

% Angle that the door makes with the floor 
theta = 30;  % degrees

% Magnitude of the forces
F_A = 300; % Newtons
F_C = 250; % Newtons
%[text] a) What are the coordinates of the point $B = (B\_x,B\_y,B\_z)${"altText":"B = (B\_x,B\_y,B\_z)"} in terms of the measurements `d_1`, `d_2`, `d_3`, `d_4` and `theta`?
flag = [0 0 0 0];
B_x = -0.5; %[control:editfield:49da]{"position":[7,11]}
B_y = 0; %[control:editfield:7412]{"position":[7,8]}
B_z = 0; %[control:editfield:3777]{"position":[7,8]}
B = [B_x,B_y,B_z];
flag(1) = checkExercise1a(B,d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4,theta,F_A,F_C);   %[control:button:4492]{"position":[61,62]} %[output:536e78b7]
%%
%[text] b) What are the coordinates of the point $A = (A\_x,A\_y,A\_z)${"altText":"A = (A\_x,A\_y,A\_z)"} in terms of the measurements `d_1`, `d_2`, `d_3`, `d_4` and `theta`?
A_x = 0; %[control:editfield:84d5]{"position":[7,8]}
A_y = -(1+1.5*cosd(30)); %[control:editfield:6940]{"position":[7,24]}
A_z = 1.5*sind(30); %[control:editfield:3c02]{"position":[7,19]}
A = [A_x,A_y,A_z];
if ~exist("flag","var")
    flag = [-1 0 0 0];
end
flag(2) = checkExercise1b(A,d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4,theta,F_A,F_C);     %[control:button:32e0]{"position":[63,64]} %[output:3f3b7534]
%%
%[text] c) What are the coordinates of the point $ C = (C\_x,C\_y,C\_z)${"altText":"C = (C\_x,C\_y,C\_z)"} in terms of the measurements `d_1`, `d_2`, `d_3`, `d_4` and `theta`?
C_x =  -2.5; %[control:editfield:1811]{"position":[7,12]}
C_y =  -(1+1.5*cosd(30)); %[control:editfield:3ce8]{"position":[7,25]}
C_z =  1.5*sind(30); %[control:editfield:665b]{"position":[7,20]}
C = [C_x,C_y,C_z];     %[control:button:9db1]{"position":[22,23]}
if ~exist("flag","var")
    flag = [-1 -1 0 0];
end
flag(3) = checkExercise1c(C,d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4,theta,F_A,F_C); %[output:1d6445bf]
%%
%[text] d) What are the coordinates of the point $O = (O\_x,O\_y,O\_z)${"altText":"O = (O\_x,O\_y,O\_z)"} in terms of the measurements `d_1`, `d_2`, `d_3`, `d_4` and `theta`?
O_x = 0; %[control:editfield:588b]{"position":[7,8]}
O_y = 0; %[control:editfield:5d09]{"position":[7,8]}
O_z =  0; %[control:editfield:311a]{"position":[7,9]}
O = [O_x,O_y,O_z];
if ~exist("flag","var")
    flag = [-1 -1 -1 0];
end
flag(4) = checkExercise1d(O,d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4,theta,F_A,F_C);      %[control:button:7d0f]{"position":[64,65]} %[output:1073b9fa]
%%
%[text] e) What are the cartesian coordinates of the force vectors in Newtons in terms of `A`, `B`, `C`, and `O`?
F_A =  300*(B-A)/norm(B-A); %[control:editfield:7169]{"position":[7,27]}
F_C = 150*(O-C)/norm(O-C);      %[control:editfield:7a37]{"position":[7,26]} %[control:button:814d]{"position":[31,32]}
checkExercise1e(A,B,C,O,flag,1e-2,F_A,F_C); %[output:2e9b29ab]
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7f42) **Reflect**. Are there any forces missing from this diagram?
% SOLUTION
%[text] Yes, if this door is supposed to be in equlibrium, the sum of the forces must balance. It currently does not. Also, the door is not weightless, so the force of gravity is also missing from this diagram. If gravity is in the $-z${"altText":"-z"} direction, there must be other forces missing as well. Perhaps forces on the hinges of the door. 
Fweight = F_A + F_C
% END SOLUTION
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DBC5EC15] ## Conditions of Equilibrium
%[text] There must be no displacement and no rotation. Mathematically, this is that the sum of all forces and the sum of all moments are both zero.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle \\sum F = 0${"altText":"sum F = 0"}      and      $\\displaystyle \\sum M = 0${"altText":"sum M = 0"}.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2EAB2EB9] ### ![](text:image:770b) **Exercise 2: Calculate Tensions in 3 Dimensions**
%[text] A box weighing 150 pounds, point E, is suspended below point A by three cables attached at points B, C, and D, as shown below. Determine the tension in each cable.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Points B and C are in a vertical supporting wall, while points A and D are in a line perpendicular to the supporting wall. A is a distance d\_5 from the wall and D is a distance d\_6 from the wall. Measuring from the point where the AD line intersects the wall, B is right a distance d\_3 and up a distance d\_4 while C is left a distance d\_2 and up a distance d\_1.](text:image:5428)
%[text] Assume the distances are measured as follows:
d_1 = 3; % feet
d_2 = 2; % feet
d_3 = 5; % feet
d_4 = 2; % feet
d_5 = 6; % feet
d_6 = 7; % feet
%[text] Define the weight of the box in pounds.
W = 150;    %[control:editfield:4515]{"position":[5,8]}
%[text] Identify the coordinates of the points A, B, C, and D in terms of the measured distances `d_1`, `d_2`, `d_3`, `d_4`, `d_5`, and `d_6`. Does it matter which point you choose to be the origin?
A = [0 0 0];    %[control:editfield:42ff]{"position":[6,11]}
B = [d_5 d_3 d_4]; %[control:editfield:3462]{"position":[6,17]}
C = [d_5 -d_2 d_1]; %[control:editfield:5af0]{"position":[6,18]}
D = [ d_5-d_6 0 0 ]; %[control:editfield:3190]{"position":[6,19]}
%[text] Identify vectors along the relevant cables in terms of `A`, `B`, `C`, and `D`.
AB = B-A; %[control:editfield:480b]{"position":[6,9]}
AC = C-A; %[control:editfield:4b1f]{"position":[6,9]}
AD = D-A; %[control:editfield:0518]{"position":[6,9]}
%[text] Normalize the vectors along the cables in terms of `A`, `B`, `C`, and `D`.
N_AB = AB/norm(AB);  %[control:editfield:4776]{"position":[8,19]}
N_AC = AC/norm(AC); %[control:editfield:65c1]{"position":[8,19]}
N_AD =  AD/norm(AD); %[control:editfield:8376]{"position":[8,20]}
%[text] Define symbolic variables representing the tension in each cable and a symbolic unit system.
syms F_AC F_AB F_AD
u = symunit;
%[text] Define a system of equations that balances the forces in each direction in terms of `F_AC`, `F_AB`, and `F_AD` as well as the normalized vectors and other forces in the problem. 
balance_x = F_AB*N_AB(1)+F_AC*N_AC(1)+F_AD*N_AD(1) == 0; %[control:editfield:9fc6]{"position":[13,56]}
balance_y = F_AB*N_AB(2)+F_AC*N_AC(2)+F_AD*N_AD(2) == 0; %[control:editfield:0619]{"position":[13,56]}
balance_z = F_AB*N_AB(3)+F_AC*N_AC(3)+F_AD*N_AD(3) == W; %[control:editfield:437c]{"position":[13,56]}
%[text] Solve the system of equations and display the results.
[F_AC, F_AB, F_AD]=solve([balance_x,balance_y,balance_z],[F_AC,F_AB,F_AD]);
outVal = checkEx2(F_AC,F_AB,F_AD,d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4,d_5,d_6,W);
if outVal
F_AC = round(F_AC,2)*u.lbf
F_AB = round(F_AB,2)*u.lbf
F_AD = round(F_AD,2)*u.lbf
end
